  "text": "Neither solution's final answer holds up.\n\n**Solution 1 (answer 1)** claims order 1 is impossible for any non-trivial universe, but the universe containing only the zero game gives a one-element quotient. The sum-to-1 conclusion depends on the claim that order 2 is realized, and that is the weak point. Its Klein-group argument is hand-waving, and its odd-order construction is never carried out. Even if order 1 were excluded, its answer would still be wrong unless every other order were realized.\n\n**Solution 2 (answer 0)** asserts that every positive integer is realized, with no construction for orders 3 and up. It also treats order 2 as easy. I recall work on partisan misère quotients (Carvalho, Huggan, Siegel, Sopena) indicating that some small orders, plausibly including 2, are not realized, so the sum is likely positive. I can't check that paper here, so this is recalled rather than verified.\n\nBoth solutions reduce to a claim about which small orders occur, and neither proves it. If order 2 is in fact impossible, the answer would be 2, and neither solution gives that. I can't confirm the true set of unrealizable orders, but I don't see grounds to accept either 0 or 1.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
